 - **Willy Maker** (`/tools/willy-maker`, a card in Tools, in English, Spanish and Portuguese): a visual maker of CPS-1 arcade games for go-link, built as a self-contained module (`src/willy-maker/`). The home screen lists the games saved in this browser (open, duplicate, download, delete) and opens `.zip` projects; a three-step wizard picks the board (CPS-1), the layout (4 players × 3 or × 2 buttons) and a start: the **Buenos Aires** template (Mission 1's docks, cranes, tower lobby, server floors and helipad on an 8192 × 672 canvas, drawn with the ROM prototype's street tiles), an empty level, or a Tiled map. The **Build** screen is an editor like an IDE: a canvas with zoom, pan, grid and the 384 × 224 screen frame; a pencil, eraser and fill that paint collision tags (solid, platform, ladder, crate, breakable, hazard, water) with automatic street art, or tiles; stamps for crates, pickups, enemies, bosses, civilians, player starts, checkpoints, camera locks and the exit, each with a unique reference name and its properties in the inspector; image-editor layers (show, lock, rename, order, opacity, add); undo and redo; a minimap with the level's sections; the CPS-1's limits as meters; and a reachability check with the prototype's rules (64 px jumps, 32 px crates, ladders, one-way ledges) that marks the ledges and objects nobody can reach. Projects save themselves in the browser (localStorage, pictures in IndexedDB) and export to a `.zip` (project.json, the pictures by hash, a thumbnail per level) that opens in another browser; Export lists the checks first. Characters and Play open from the same top bar.
 - **Willy Maker › Characters** (`src/willy-maker/sprites/`, in English, Spanish and Portuguese): drop a sprite sheet and the frames are found on their own: the background is keyed from the sheet's border (a black shirt inside the outline stays), a magenta background everywhere, separator lines and label text are left out, a muzzle flash joins its figure and poses that only touch are split; or cut the sheet by a fixed grid. The boxes can be selected, moved, resized, nudged with the arrows, deleted or drawn by hand, with the pivot on the feet. Frames go to the art spec's animations for the role (with each one's expected frame count), with frame rate and loop, or to new ones. One scale brings the character to its height on screen (44 px heroes and the other art-spec presets); the palette zones (16 px rows from the feet: head, torso, legs) show their colors against the 15 allowed, snapped to the board's 4096 colors in OKLab with the delta E shown, reduced to the 15 nearest when over, with a hint naming the two most alike colors; a click marks the shirt colors for players 2 to 4. The animation plays at 1x and 4x exactly as the board shows it. Saving packs the frames 1:1 into the character's picture, adds its zone palettes to the project and keeps the dropped sheet and the boxes so it can be edited again.
 - **Willy Maker › Game and Menus** (`src/willy-maker/game/`, in English, Spanish and Portuguese): the Game tab sets the players (up to the board layout's count), the actions the layout fixes with editable labels (B1 jump, B2 fire with the automatic knife, B3 special, or B1+B2 on `captcomm`; the double-tap run window, 250 ms by default), each player's character (Willy and three recruit shirts by default), "Your controller" (the detected controller lit while pressed, press-to-assign remapping of the keyboard and each controller model in the site's button map, the on-screen pad choice for play mode, go-link defaults) and the DIP switches. The Menus tab designs the title, demo, player select, HUD, continue, game over and high-score screens with a live 384 × 224 preview in the board's 8 × 8 font (now in `@go-link/cps1`, shared with the ROM tools), text fields, background, music slot and credits line, and checks that the text fits the screen and its safe area and uses only the font's glyphs. Play mode uses the run window, the 2-button special, the shirts, the HUD texts and draws the Game over screen in the board font. New live rules (missing characters, empty title, text overflow, unsupported letters) appear in the Game tab, the Build warnings and the Export review. Projects move to format 2; format 1 games are migrated on load.
